[#:seon.task{:name "failure-evidence-is-stored-as-text"
             :plan "`:seon.test.failure/expected` and :actual are declared :string and `:seon.test/failure-message` is a stored string read in nine places (test.clj, issue.clj, render/test.clj, test/accretion.clj, problems.clj, sci/eval.clj), so report-value, message-text and failure-message cannot retire (R10). Store native operands (A/B key retirement) and convert the readers. Read `seon.test.runner/record-tx` and `docs/research/agent-platform/landing/delete-r8-r6-r10-2026-09-26.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-rerun-failure-report-conflicts-and-faults-the-request"
             :plan "Root's central test runs on default recorded seven :seon.test/report-conflict refusals of record-tx as core faults (four signatures, 2026-09-26 23:04Z to 2026-09-27 01:56Z): a failing test whose captured report has the same identity (test, failure identity, reported position) as a stored one but different facts is refused, and the whole completion write becomes a fault instead of the test's red. A re-reported failure is evidence of the same claim, never a conflict: either its differing facts belong in the identity or they are not stored on the report. Read `seon.test.runner/record-tx`, `seon.test.runner/report-entity` and `docs/research/agent-platform/landing/stability-opus-2026-09-27.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "reach-over-every-test-takes-thirty-seconds"
             :plan "`seon.test.runner/reach-memberships` over all 2,377 test entities took 31.6 s on default (2026-09-27, warm, one call): over the ten-second bound. Since test-feedback-perf (2026-09-27) every requested test's closure is derived together and each digest is cached under its closure's fingerprint: all 2,378 digests answer in 951 ms when cached, but the first derivation in a JVM took 17 s, about 5 ms a test printing its closure for `seon.id/digest`. Done when the first whole-suite derivation is under one second: stream each closure's printed parts into the digest instead of printing the whole vector (seon.id owns the printed form). Read `seon.test.runner/reach-digests` and `docs/research/agent-platform/test-agents-concurrency-2026-09-27.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "drift-is-blamed-on-concurrent-requests"
             :plan "With two test requests running at once in one JVM, ambient-drift blames one request's member for the other's instrumentation changes: refusal-test/every-producer-references-the-worlds-it-was-handed went red with 486 foreign wrappers while instrument-test ran; `seon.error.refusal-test/armed-refusal-returns-every-declared-family-without-refusing` red with :snapshot-planned drift-removed while a second refusal-test request ran. restore-live-cluster-schema! also advances every live cluster's projection after every member. The before/after snapshot assumes nothing else runs; test agents (option B) route JVM-global tests to the isolated host and delete this family (seon.instrument/instrumentation-is-jvm-global). Done when concurrent requests of non-host-global tests produce no drift reds. Read `seon.test.runner/ambient-drift`, `seon.test.runner/ambient-snapshot`, `seon.test.runner/restore-live-cluster-schema!` and `docs/research/agent-platform/test-agents-concurrency-2026-09-27.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-green-re-record-sweeps-the-failure-cell"
             :plan "Recording a green result after a red one refuses vector-element-swept on `:seon.test/failures`: the runner retracts the old failure entity while the vector cell naming it stays (reproduced at e999fdd1b, E4; test-failure-facts-test recorded-reach-belongs-to-the-tested-value-and-is-replaced, what-made-it-red-names-changed-functions). Replace the failures vector through its owner in the same transaction instead of retracting its elements. Read `seon.test.runner/commit-results!` and `docs/research/agent-platform/red-triage-r6-2026-09-27.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "reach-entries-fill-half-the-cache-at-half-hit-rate"
             :plan "seon.cache kind :seon.test.runner/reach-entries holds 288 MB of the 512 MB budget for 33 loads (about 8.7 MB per entry) at a 49% hit rate, 15.1 s total load (default 2026-10-07, cache-status). It evicts the rest of the cache: :seon.schema.datahike/decoded-edn 41,601 misses and 21,743 evictions, reach-digest hit 35%. An entry is a whole-program reach map per key; find what each entry holds that its consumer reads and store only that (or key it by content so consecutive runs share it). Coordinate with lane no-print-hash (reach digests). Done when reach-entries stays under 10% of the budget with a hit rate above 90% across two consecutive test runs; regression: a runner test asserting the entry's weighed bytes for a small program. Read `seon.cache/status`, `seon.test.runner/reach-entries` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/waste-rank--cache-kinds.txt`."}
 #:seon.task{:name
            "a-test-error-shows-runner-frames-not-the-throw-site",
            :plan
            "A deftest whose body calls an agent function that throws (scratch probe-g throwing (ex-info \"inside test\" {:probe/x 3})) is reported by `my.test/check` with `:seon.test.failure/actual` as printed text whose 30 frames are seon.test.runner/seon.sci.eval/seon.test frames: the throw site (probe-g, its SCI location) appears nowhere, and the same text is printed again into the reply's :out (~733 tokens). Correct: the error inside a test is the same flat error an evaluation answers (ex-data whole, the author's SCI stacktrace), stored as data, never printed to :out. Linked: seon.test.runner/failure-evidence-is-stored-as-text (text storage). Proof: a check of a throwing test whose failure names the throwing function's SCI frame. Read `seon.test.runner/record-tx`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "verified-recomputes-reach-digests-for-the-whole-program"
             :plan "seon.test/verified? on a proof with a recorded result calls seon.test.runner/reach-digests: 5.9 s, 4.2 GB, 3.3M calls cold (whole-program work) on every seon.task/assign! of a proof with evidence. Done when the digests are derived only for the proof's reach and cached by content, with timing on the same probe under one second. Read `seon.test.runner/reach-digests` and `seon.test/verified?`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "tests-reaching-rebuilds-the-whole-program-reach-graph-per-change"
             :plan "After any program change, the first `seon.test.runner/tests-reaching` over every test rebuilds the reach graph of the whole program (about 9,000 names and 6,000 schema nodes): 280 ms and 671 MB with the behavior digests cold, 130 ms and 217 MB warm, on a default value with one changed attribute (default 667cc9a95, 2026-10-09). An eval request whose defn no test reaches spent 690 ms in its test step, and whoever reads reach first after a change pays it (one task's proofs, a check's selection, verified?). `seon.test.runner/reach-declarations` also holds 230 MB, 45% of the cache, as whole-program maps per revision set. A frontier walk through `seon.db` reads is no fix: 24 rounds for a 753-name closure cost 21 ms and 50 MB, and 50 tests 195 ms and 480 MB. Done when the tests reaching a change are found from the changed names by the indexed reverse edges (`:seon.fn/calls`, `:seon.fn/references` and `:seon.test/subject` are AVET-indexed), or the previous graph is advanced by the changed declarations, with a `seon.profile/measure` proof that an eval request reaching nothing reads in proportion to its change. Read `seon.test.runner/reach-graph` and `seon.turn/run-reaching`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "tests-reaching-misses-seon-id-id-from-its-calling-test"
             :plan "`seon.test.reach-test/tests-reaching-answers-the-tests-whose-closure-holds-the-target` is red at default f54e73c83 without any branch change (run 45031177abb8, 2026-10-09; also red in the check of c5826eb62's candidate, which does not touch seon.test.runner): `(seon.test.runner/tests-reaching database [seon.id-test/data-shape-and-explicit-length-determine-identity seon.db-test/semantic-replay-declines-opaque-and-lazy-values-without-realizing] 'seon.id/id)` answers `#{}` while `seon.test.runner/reach-memberships` of the same tests holds `seon.id/id` for the first. Either `seon.test.runner/reach-graph`'s reversed edges drop the path memberships follow, or the test's assumption is retired; classify by the commit that changed it and fix the owner or the test. Done when that test is green. Read `seon.test.runner/tests-reaching`."}

 #:seon.task{:name "a-private-callee-of-a-reached-function-is-unreached"
             :plan "`my.program/tests-reaching` of `seon.cluster.source/write-back!` lists 11 gating tests, but `seon.cluster.source/proven-file` (called by `seon.cluster.source/proven-files`, called by `seon.cluster.source/write-back-commits`, called by write-back!) has none, so `bin/lane evidence` answers `unreached [seon.cluster.source/proven-file]` and a REPL-proven write-back change must take the throwaway check (commit 31b442840, 2026-10-10); `my.test/check` with it changed answers `:seon.test/unreached`. Done when the reach of a test calling write-back! includes proven-file. Read `seon.cluster.branch-evidence/branch-evidence`, `seon.fn/tests-reaching` and `seon.test.runner/tests-reaching`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-forked-value-reads-the-results-it-inherited"
  :plan "Phase P1 of the evidence (F1). `seon.test.runner/latest-results` keeps only runs whose `:seon.test.run/branch` is the reading value's own `[:config :branch]`, so a value forked from a branch (a test member's, a successor attempt's) answers nothing for the runs it inherited: reproduced on default 2319152dc (scratch agent hybrid-steps-probe): after one `my.test/check`, latest-results on the branch answers [[sym 1 0]] and, with only [:config :branch] renamed on the same value, []. A Datahike branch value holds exactly its lineage's commits and `seon.context/proof-status` already compares the reach digest, so the clause is a stamp beside the key that decides validity: delete it, and the same clause in `seon.test/changed-since-green`. Safety: `seon.turn/step-proved?` means 'this attempt's step is proved': results at their current reach, so an inherited result counts only at the program the attempt works on. Proofs: seon.test.runner-test/a-forked-value-reads-the-results-it-inherited (this probe as a test); seon.turn-test/a-predecessors-proof-run-does-not-count-as-the-attempts. Files: src/seon/test/runner.clj, src/seon/test.clj, src/seon/turn.clj, test/seon/test/runner_test.clj, test/seon/turn_test.clj. Read `seon.context/proofs`, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/chain-experiment-1.md` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-step-semantics.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-first-reach-digest-after-a-change-costs-what-changed"
             :plan "(hybrid-test-results R11) The key costs what changed. `seon.test.runner/reach-attribute` derives a new revision's map from the previous one plus the datoms of `seon.db/entities-changed-after` its basis instead of re-reading the whole attribute range, and `seon.test.runner/reached-graph` takes every reach fact (`:seon.fn/calls`, `/references`, `/keywords`, `/spec`, `/doc`, `:seon.test/subject`) from those attribute maps as `seon.test.runner/reach-declarations` already does for names and digests, so visiting a node reads nothing; component digests stay a Merkle hash and `::reach-index` stays keyed by content. Batching the pulls alone stays red (cost-gb: 481 ms / 1,246 MB). Red proof: `seon.test.reach-test/a-changed-programs-first-reach-digest-is-subsecond` (cost-gb's proof, saved in `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/cost-gb-reach.patch`, re-added): after one declaration transaction, `seon.test.runner/reach-digests` for one real test is under 100 ms and 128 MB through `seon.profile/measure` (HEAD: 605 ms / 1,523 MB); the whole-program derivation after one change is reported beside the bounds. Related and not replaced: `seon.test.runner/tests-reaching-rebuilds-the-whole-program-reach-graph-per-change`. Files: `src/seon/test/runner.clj`, `test/seon/test/reach_test.clj`. Read `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-test-results.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-cited-test-render-run-throws-a-null-future-in-run-selected-tests"
             :plan "`seon.render-test-summary-test/a-cited-test-shows-its-source-and-state-and-a-listed-one-stays-compact` is red on HEAD (c605003b5 and 249c18f9a, reported by lane host-memo; it fails the same on a clean agent branch) with a NullPointerException \"Cannot invoke Future.get() because fut is null\" raised under `seon.test.runner/run-selected-tests`, reached from `seon.test.runner/run-vars!` through `seon.db/with-branch` when the test's `fixture-run` calls `(seon.test.runner/run-vars! vars {})` with an empty custody (no `:seon.db/connection`, no `:seon.sci.eval/ctx`). The message is not first-party text: the same string is quoted in `seon.mcp-entrance-test/a-cluster-still-starting-or-stopping-refuses-by-name` as the bare NullPointerException an eval answered while a cluster's registry held a partial instance, so a Future that was never created (a nil in the position a started execution belongs) is the suspect. Not reproduced by this filing (the test namespace is not loaded in the JVM; run the one test to get the trace). Done when the cause is isolated at its producer (the empty custody reaching `run-selected-tests`, or the missing Future), the test is green with its assertions on the rendered data, and a custody-less `run-vars!` returns the captured results or a refusal by name, never a bare NullPointerException. Proof: `seon.render-test-summary-test/a-cited-test-shows-its-source-and-state-and-a-listed-one-stays-compact` (red today). Read `seon.test.runner/run-selected-tests`, `seon.test.runner/run-vars!` and `seon.db/with-branch`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "reaching-tests-assert-what-the-runner-answers-not-cache-retention"
             :plan "`seon.test-reaching-test/reach-digests-follow-only-changed-closures` (line 117, `(some? read)`) and `seon.test-reaching-test/two-branches-of-one-program-share-reach-declarations` (line 131, `(some? content)`) read `seon.cache/retained` of `:seon.test.runner/reach-attribute` through their `retained-declarations` helper, so they are red in a broad run whose earlier tests' load evicts those entries and green alone: on the S2a slice red in check run c9009a13d64b (118 tests), green alone in f93113751df5, and green alone on HEAD in 69cafee91ff5 (2026-10-14). The test is wrong, not the runner: assert on what `seon.test.runner/reach-digests` answers and on the derivation count the runner reports, never on what the central cache happens to retain. Done when both pass in a broad run of `seon.test-reaching-test` beside `seon.task-test` and their asserted behavior (a data write derives nothing; a new branch reuses the parent's declarations) is still proven."
             :proofs #{seon.test-reaching-test/reach-digests-follow-only-changed-closures seon.test-reaching-test/two-branches-of-one-program-share-reach-declarations}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-timeout-red-names-the-testing-context-in-flight"
             :plan "A proof red by the runner's time limit (\"did not exit within :seon.test/check-time-limit-ms remainder 4396 ms; thread 241 remains live\") names no `testing` context or row; outside-1 (`docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/outside-1.md`, Misleading item 1, call 7) probed three producers in one body for 34,772 ms to find that the `slow` row was the one running. Done when the timeout's error map carries the `clojure.test/*testing-contexts*` in flight when the limit fired, proven by a runner test whose body sleeps inside a named `testing`. Read `seon.test.runner`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-timed-out-result-reused-by-reach-digest-leaves-no-assertions-to-read"
             :plan "After a test timed out, `(clojure.test/run-test-var ...)` answered the reused timeout red (`:seon.test/reused-count 1`) and the assertion failures stayed unreachable while the timeout result held the reach digest (outside-1, `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/outside-1.md`, call 4): an agent has no way to see what the body asserted short of editing the test. Done when a timed-out result is not reused as the current result for a changed question, or the reply carries what the body recorded before the limit, proven by a runner row. Read `seon.test.runner` and `seon.test.cache`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-mixed-path-result-keys-what-ran"
             :plan "Reuse is keyed by `[sym reach-digest input-digest]` (no evaluator, owner ruling 2026-10-15, branch-model rulings 6), so the reach digest must name what actually ran. A branch test reached through both an interpreted path and a host-bound caller (`seon.sci.eval/host-bound-row?`; `seon.fn/closure-walk` stops there since 57e359aa9) runs that caller's callees as main's loaded seed, yet `seon.test.runner/reach-digests` keys it with the branch's definitions of those callees, so after the merge main could reuse a result part of which never saw the change. Count a host-bound caller's callees at their loaded digests (the loaded commit, `seon.sci.eval/loaded-identity`) in `seon.test.runner/task-reach-digests`: one lookup per host-bound node, so a run with a compiled path gets a digest the candidate and main do not share, and a run with none keeps an equal digest and is reused. Red proof to write: a row beside `seon.merge-test/a-merge-reuses-the-chain-branchs-green-at-an-unchanged-reach` where the seed reaches the branch change through a host-bound caller too, asserting the candidate does not reuse the branch's run (`:seon.test.member/reused-run` absent). Read `seon.test/carried-results` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-15/merge-closure-plan.md#phases-each-with-its-red-proof`."}]
